    "result_text": "Green. I agreed with both findings and applied the preferred fix.\n\n## What changed\n\nI replaced the nested `if`/`else if` chain in `TelephoneNormalizer` with a declarative lookup table:\n\n```java\nprivate static final Map<String, Integer> NATIONAL_LENGTH = Map.of(\"61\", 9, \"1\", 10);\n...\nfor (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : NATIONAL_LENGTH.entrySet()) {\n    String countryCode = entry.getKey();\n    if (digits.startsWith(countryCode)\n            && digits.length() - countryCode.length() != entry.getValue()) {\n        return null;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis addresses both findings at once:\n- **Country \u2192 length is now data, not code.** Adding a country is a new map entry, not a new branch with two more magic numbers. The \"subtract the country-code length\" step is derived from `countryCode.length()` rather than hardcoded per branch.\n- **The dead nesting is gone** \u2014 the condition is flat and combined, so it no longer reads as if it should have an `else`.\n\nBehaviour is identical: the two country codes never overlap as prefixes (`\"61\"` never starts with `\"1\"`, and `\"1\"`-numbers never start with `\"6\"`), so at most one entry matches \u2014 same outcome as the original chain. The Javadoc I added earlier still accurately describes the rule, and the map's own comment notes that unlisted codes keep the generic 8\u201315 rule.\n\nFull suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` acceptance test.",
