    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe logic lives in the right unit \u2014 `TelephoneNormalizer` already owns length rules, so per-country length checks belong here, and the change is additive with matching Javadoc. But the shape of the addition isn't idiomatic and is the seed of a catch-all:\n\n- **Country \u2192 required-length knowledge is encoded as code, not data.** The `if (startsWith(\"61\")) \u2026 else if (startsWith(\"1\")) \u2026` chain, with the country-code length baked into each `length() - 2` / `length() - 1` and the required national count baked into `!= 9` / `!= 10`, means every new country adds another branch plus two more magic numbers. A small table expresses the same rule declaratively and doesn't grow the method:\n  ```java\n  // country code -> required national-number length\n  Map<String, Integer> nationalLength = Map.of(\"61\", 9, \"1\", 10);\n  for (var e : nationalLength.entrySet()) {\n      if (digits.startsWith(e.getKey())\n          && digits.length() - e.getKey().length() != e.getValue()) {\n          return null;\n      }\n  }\n  ```\n  This also removes the duplicated \"subtract the country-code length\" step that currently appears once per branch as a hardcoded constant.\n\n- **The nested `if`s carry no weight.** Each inner block only does `return null`, so\n  ```java\n  if (digits.startsWith(\"61\")) {\n      if (digits.length() - 2 != 9) { return null; }\n  }\n  ```\n  should just be a single combined condition `if (digits.startsWith(\"61\") && digits.length() - 2 != 9) return null;`. As written the extra nesting reads like it should have an `else`, which it doesn't.\n\nEither flatten the conditions or (preferably) move the country/length pairs into a lookup so the method stays flat as more countries are inevitably added.",
